CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure shows two identical cubic crystal lattices drawn side by side in perspective. Each is a three-by-three-by-three array of small red spheres standing for atoms, joined by thin black lines tracing the cube edges and the rows between nearest neighbors, so the block reads as open and transparent. In the left lattice, a translucent teal plane lies parallel to one pair of cube faces and passes through the middle sheet of atoms, appearing darker where it sits behind the front layer of spheres and paler where it emerges near the top face. In the right lattice, the highlighted plane is tilted instead, slicing diagonally from the lower left of the cube toward the upper right and intersecting a different set of spheres, its shading again splitting between a darker interior portion and a lighter one near the top. Holding the lattice fixed while changing only the orientation of the shaded plane makes that orientation the single variable in view. The pair conveys that one crystal holds many families of parallel atomic planes at differing angles and spacings, each able to produce its own X-ray diffraction.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - 27 small red spheres arranged as a 3x3x3 simple-cubic lattice (atoms), each with a dark red rim/specular highlight
  - thin black straight lines joining every pair of nearest-neighbour spheres along the three cube axes (3 sheets x 3 lines in each of x, y, z), forming the open wireframe grid
  - outer cube edges of the lattice, drawn slightly heavier than interior rows
  - one translucent teal plane cutting through the lattice, rendered double-sided
  - darker teal region of the plane where it lies behind the front layer of spheres, paler teal where it emerges past the top/side faces (depth-dependent shading)
  - the sub-set of red spheres that actually lie in the highlighted plane, drawn emphasised (slightly larger / brighter) so the learner sees which atoms the plane contains
  - Miller-index caption for the current plane family, e.g. (100), (110), (111)
  - interplanar spacing label d = a / sqrt(h^2+k^2+l^2) with its numeric value
  - faint ghost outlines of the other parallel planes of the same family, so the family reads as a stack

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Lattice constant a = 1 world unit; atoms sit at integer coordinates x,y,z ∈ {-1, 0, 1}, so the cube spans ±1 (2×2×2 world units overall) and is centred on the origin. Sphere radius ≈ 0.10 (about 1/10 of the edge), highlighted in-plane spheres radius ≈ 0.13. Wireframe: black (#1a1a1a) lines of ~1.5 px joining nearest neighbours along all three axes; outer 12 cube edges ~2 px. Colours read from source: spheres bright vermilion #E8332A with a darker maroon rim (#8f1f18) and a small white specular dot upper-left; interior (occluded) portion of the plane deep teal #4E9E95 at ~0.65 opacity; the portion emerging past the top/near faces pale teal #CFE7E3 at ~0.35 opacity — emulate with double-sided material plus depth-based tint, not two separate meshes. Background white. Camera: three-quarter view from slightly above and to the left, the classic textbook cube pose — front-left vertical face nearly facing the viewer, right face receding, top face visible; mild perspective (fov ≈ 30°) so the far edges converge only slightly. Plane geometry: for Miller indices (hkl) the plane is n·r = m·d with unit normal n = (h,k,l)/|(h,k,l)|, d = a/√(h²+k²+l²), m = planeIndex. (100): normal +X, a vertical sheet parallel to the left/right cube faces, passing through x = planeIndex — this matches the LEFT panel of the source, where the sheet sits in the mid-plane through the central column of atoms. (110): normal (1,1,0)/√2, a vertical sheet tilted 45° that cuts from the lower-left front edge up to the upper-right back edge — this matches the RIGHT panel's diagonal slice. (111): normal (1,1,1)/√3, a triangular/hexagonal cut across three corners. Clip each plane to the cube: render it as the convex polygon of intersection with the cube slightly oversized (×1.08) so a thin rim pokes past the faces, as in the source. Labels: Miller index caption and 'd = a/√(h²+k²+l²) = 1.000 a / 0.707 a / 0.577 a' in small dark grey sans-serif below the cube.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitLattice",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The teal sheet is a genuine 2D plane threading a 3D lattice, not a painted rectangle; rotating shows which atoms really lie in it and how the plane edge-on becomes a line of atoms."
  },
  {
    "id": "planeFamily",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Plane family (Miller indices)",
    "options": [
      "(100) face-parallel",
      "(110) diagonal",
      "(111) body-diagonal"
    ],
    "default": "(100) face-parallel",
    "teaches": "One and the same crystal contains several families of parallel atomic planes at different angles; only the plane orientation changes while the red-sphere lattice stays fixed."
  },
  {
    "id": "planeIndex",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Which plane in the stack",
    "range": [
      -1,
      1,
      1
    ],
    "default": 0,
    "teaches": "Stepping to the next parallel plane of the family shifts the sheet by exactly the interplanar spacing d and highlights a different sheet of atoms — the d that enters Bragg's law."
  },
  {
    "id": "showStack",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Show whole parallel stack",
    "default": false,
    "teaches": "The family is an evenly spaced stack: every atom in the crystal lies on some plane of the family, and the spacing shrinks as the Miller indices grow."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Left panel: planes parallel to a cube face",
    "narration": "The teal sheet lies parallel to one pair of cube faces and passes through the central sheet of nine atoms. This is the (100) family, spacing d = a.",
    "state": {
      "planeFamily": "(100) face-parallel",
      "planeIndex": 0,
      "showStack": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Step to the neighbouring plane",
    "narration": "Sliding to the next plane translates the sheet by one lattice constant and lights up the front layer of atoms instead — successive planes of the same family are separated by d.",
    "state": {
      "planeFamily": "(100) face-parallel",
      "planeIndex": 1,
      "showStack": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The whole (100) stack",
    "narration": "Showing all three planes at once reveals that every atom in the crystal belongs to one plane of this evenly spaced family.",
    "state": {
      "planeFamily": "(100) face-parallel",
      "planeIndex": 0,
      "showStack": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Right panel: diagonal (110) planes",
    "narration": "Now the same lattice is cut diagonally from the lower left toward the upper right. A different set of spheres lies in the plane, and the spacing tightens to d = a/√2.",
    "state": {
      "planeFamily": "(110) diagonal",
      "planeIndex": 0,
      "showStack": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "A steeper family still",
    "narration": "The (111) planes slice across three corners; their spacing d = a/√3 is smaller again, so they diffract X-rays at different angles than the (100) or (110) planes.",
    "state": {
      "planeFamily": "(111) body-diagonal",
      "planeIndex": 0,
      "showStack": true
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 35,
  "elevationDeg": 18,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.68
}

NOTES: Build the lattice once: InstancedMesh of 27 SphereGeometry(0.10, 24, 16) with MeshStandardMaterial (red, roughness 0.35) and a LineSegments for all nearest-neighbour bonds. Plane: compute the cube–plane intersection polygon analytically (Sutherland–Hodgman clip of a large quad against the 6 cube half-spaces) and rebuild BufferGeometry on family/index change; MeshBasicMaterial with transparent:true, side:DoubleSide, depthWrite:false, and render after the spheres so the teal correctly darkens where it sits behind atoms. For the two-tone look, use a custom shader (or vertex colours along the plane's local up axis) that lerps opacity/colour from deep teal at the lower/interior region to pale teal near the top exit edge. Highlight in-plane atoms by testing |n·p - m·d| < 1e-3 and scaling those instances up with an emissive boost. Clamp planeIndex range per family so the plane always intersects the cube ((100): -1..1, (110): -1..1, (111): -1..1 in units of d, rounded to planes that actually hit atoms). Animate plane translation with a short tween (~250 ms) so the slider reads as sliding through the stack. OrbitControls with damping, no zoom past the cube, target at origin.

Follow the interaction plan above.

